<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">5 October in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">5 events that happened on 5 October, from 869 to 1962.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">869</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Fourth Council of Constantinople opens</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">A church council opened in Constantinople that deposed the patriarch Photius. Counted by the Catholic Church as the eighth ecumenical council but rejected by the Orthodox, it foreshadowed the later schism between East and West.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1762</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Gluck's Orfeo ed Euridice premieres in Vienna</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Christoph Willibald Gluck's Orfeo ed Euridice was first performed at the Burgtheater in Vienna. Its simplicity and dramatic directness began Gluck's reform of opera away from showy vocal display.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1789</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Parisian women march on Versailles</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Thousands of Parisians, led by market women angry at the price of bread, marched to Versailles. The next day they brought Louis XVI and his family back to Paris, placing the monarchy under the eye of the Revolution.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1962</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Dr. No premieres, launching the James Bond films</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Dr. No, starring Sean Connery as Ian Fleming's secret agent, premiered at the London Pavilion. It began one of the longest-running series in cinema history.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1962</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Beatles release their first single, 'Love Me Do'</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Parlophone released 'Love Me Do', the Beatles' debut single in Britain. It reached only number 17, but within a year the band from Liverpool had become a national sensation.</p></li></ol>
